systemd: drop procfs mount options this kernel cannot honour

ProtectProc= and ProcSubset= are written into a procfs mount option string by
mount_procfs(), symbolically: "hidepid=invisible", "subset=pid".  Both spellings
arrived in Linux 5.8.  Before that procfs parses hidepid= as a number and knows
no subset= at all, and it rejects the whole option string over a single element
it does not recognise, so one unknown token fails the mount with -EINVAL.  This
is 5 of the 20 units still failing at step NAMESPACE in stage-2 attempt 7; the
other 15 are 0013.

The numeric spelling is accepted.  Measured on dandelion (4.9.190) inside a
private mount namespace, mounting a fresh procfs over /mnt:

  hidepid=invisible  EINVAL
  subset=pid         EINVAL
  hidepid=1          ok
  hidepid=2          ok
  (no options)       ok

It must not be used anyway.  Per-mount hidepid arrived in the same 5.8 release
as the symbolic spelling; before it, hidepid is a field of struct pid_namespace
and is therefore shared by every procfs mount in that pid namespace.  A unit's
private mount namespace does not contain it -- only a private pid namespace
would, and units do not get one.  So mounting procfs with hidepid=2 inside
avahi-daemon's namespace sets it for PID 1's /proc as well.  Measured on the
device, with the host's mount id and superblock both unchanged across the
restart:

  # mount -o remount,hidepid=0 /proc          (in PID 1's mount namespace)
  21 29 0:4 / /proc rw,...,relatime shared:2 - proc proc rw
  # systemctl restart avahi-daemon.service
  21 29 0:4 / /proc rw,...,relatime shared:2 - proc proc rw,hidepid=2

Nothing was moved or replaced: it is one flag, reached through two mounts of one
superblock.  Four units on this device ask for ProtectProc=invisible, so the
device had been running under a system-wide hidepid=2 since the first of them
started.  The visible damage is polkit -- polkitd runs as polkituser and
resolves a D-Bus caller by reading /proc/<pid>/status, which under hidepid=2 it
cannot open for a process it does not own, so every authorization check fails
before any rule is consulted and `nmcli connection add` answers "Insufficient
privileges" to a user who is in the networkmanager group.

So retry once with no options at all when the first mount returns -EINVAL.  A
unit that asked for ProtectProc= or ProcSubset= gets a procfs with neither
applied, which is what it would have got from any kernel before 5.8, and the
exec proceeds instead of dying at step NAMESPACE.  Silently not enforcing a
hardening directive is worth stating plainly; the alternative on this kernel is
not enforcing it per-unit but enforcing it on everything.

The retry is deliberately narrow.  It only runs when the first attempt failed
with exactly -EINVAL and only when options were passed at all, so a unit with
default settings mounts procfs through the unchanged path.

Upstream is unlikely to take this: systemd's baseline is far above 4.9.

--- a/src/core/namespace.c
+++ b/src/core/namespace.c
@@ -1586,5 +1586,19 @@
          * one. i.e we don't reuse existing mounts here under any condition, we want a new instance owned by
          * our user namespace and with our hidepid= settings applied. Hence, let's get rid of everything
          * mounted on /proc/ first. */
-        return mount_private_apivfs("proc", mount_entry_path(m), "/proc", opts, p->runtime_scope);
+        r = mount_private_apivfs("proc", mount_entry_path(m), "/proc", opts, p->runtime_scope);
+        if (r != -EINVAL || !opts)
+                return r;
+
+        /* procfs rejects the whole option string over a single element it does not recognise, and both
+         * elements that can appear here arrived in the same release: the symbolic hidepid= values and
+         * subset= are Linux 5.8. The numeric hidepid= form older kernels do accept sets a flag on the pid
+         * namespace rather than on the mount, so honouring ProtectProc= through it would apply it to every
+         * procfs mount sharing that pid namespace, PID 1's included. Retry with no options instead. */
+
+        log_debug("Mounting procfs with '%s' failed with -EINVAL, retrying without options: this kernel "
+                  "predates per-mount hidepid= and subset=, so neither can be applied to this unit alone.",
+                  opts);
+
+        return mount_private_apivfs("proc", mount_entry_path(m), "/proc", /* opts= */ NULL, p->runtime_scope);
 }
